119
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:15,340
Hand a four-year-old

120
00:03:15,380 --> 00:03:16,640
a strange new toy,

121
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:18,060
she says, and the child

122
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:20,190
often beats top college students

123
00:03:20,230 --> 00:03:22,180
at figuring out how it works.

124
00:03:22,590 --> 00:03:23,990
And I think that picture,

125
00:03:24,030 --> 00:03:25,810
the picture of a system

126
00:03:25,850 --> 00:03:26,750
that develops,

127
00:03:26,790 --> 00:03:28,390
that changes over time,

128
00:03:28,430 --> 00:03:30,290
and in particular, as we'll see,

129
00:03:30,330 --> 00:03:32,570
a system that's cared for

130
00:03:32,610 --> 00:03:34,550
by humans

131
00:03:34,590 --> 00:03:35,630
or cared for by other

132
00:03:35,670 --> 00:03:37,110
intelligent agents,

133
00:03:37,150 --> 00:03:38,430
that's the secret

134
00:03:38,470 --> 00:03:39,930
of human intelligence.

135
00:03:40,450 --> 00:03:41,590
So a machine can

136
00:03:41,630 --> 00:03:43,280
hold every answer.

137
00:03:43,570 --> 00:03:44,810
A child still has

138
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:46,140
to find things out.

139
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:47,210
And for that,

140
00:03:47,250 --> 00:03:49,550
a child needs a person who cares.

141
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:50,710
But children also

142
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:52,080
learn from each other.

143
00:03:52,310 --> 00:03:54,110
And what if, by twelve,

144
00:03:54,150 --> 00:03:55,010
your child's best

145
00:03:55,050 --> 00:03:56,660
